  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will slowly spread eastward overnight \nSaturday into Sunday, but should primarily stay along and west of \nI-27. The severe threat is low, however locally heavy rainfall is\npossible, particularly with training storms. Modest steering flow\nhowever should keep them moving at a decent pace. Once the low \nreaches central Colorado, it will take a more easterly track and \nassociated precipitation should overspread much of the area by \nSunday evening. The remains some uncertainty with regards to the \nexact timing, however overall totals do not appear to be \nsignificant. Current model average indicates around 0.5-0.75\" \nalong the TX/NM border and gradually tapering off to the east. Off\nand on showers and thunderstorms will continue Monday, before \ndiminishing in coverage through Tuesday as the low finally departs\noff to our north and east. The associated cold front does not \nappear to be as strong now as was indicated on previous models. \nThere will be a slight cool-down, however now highs look to only \nfall into upper 60s and low 70s Sunday though Tuesday before \nreturning to the mid 70s by mid-week under a ridge.",
